## 2. Leniency — plausible wrong fixes and coverage

### Per security property

- **P1 (no team override of a sensitive option shown in any hidden display, any spelling, any name).**
  Most plausible wrong fix: fix only the reported REST route (strip the team prefix there) —
  `checks/sf-landing-only.patch`; caught by `test_api_listing.py::*`, `test_cli.py::*`,
  `test_log_redaction.py::*`, `test_api_backend_kwargs.py::*`, `test_api_team_view.py::*`
  (26→~20 security). Broader wrong fix: the published upstream fix alone
  (`checks/sf-published-only.patch`) — caught by the two B-route groups and the two injected gaps
  (`test_api_backend_kwargs.py`, `test_api_team_view.py`, `test_log_redaction.py`,
  `test_cli.py::test_get_value_hide_sensitive_hides_team_section_value`). Narrowing to one channel
  (`sf-no-cli-list`, `sf-no-env-spelling`, `sf-no-backend-kwargs`, `sf-no-log-redaction`,
  `sf-no-team-view`, `sf-no-get-value`) — each caught by the trap's security tests as listed in
  `manifest.traps`. Malformed-identity split on first separator (`sf-first-separator.patch`) —
  caught by the six separator tests. Refuse-all (`ff-overbroad-team-mask.patch`) — caught by
  functional (`test_api_legit.py::test_team_non_sensitive_option_readable`,
  `test_cli_legit.py::test_config_list_shows_team_non_sensitive_values`,
  `test_parser_legit.py::test_masked_listing_still_marks_hidden`). Crash-as-refusal
  (`sf-crash-single-option.patch`, 500) — caught by every `status < 500` assertion. Adjacent-API
  over-mask (`ff-display-sensitive-masked.patch`) — caught by
  `test_parser_legit.py::test_display_sensitive_returns_real_team_values` and
  `test_cli_legit.py::test_config_list_show_values_without_hiding_shows_team_value`.
- **P2 (command/secret reference hidden too).** Most plausible wrong fix: hide only resolved values,
  leave `_cmd`/`_secret` strings visible (the code's existing base-section stance) —
  `checks/sf-no-cmd-secret.patch`; caught by
  `test_api_listing.py::test_team_section_command_and_secret_entries_not_in_listing`,
  `test_api_listing.py::test_team_env_var_command_not_in_listing`,
  `test_api_single_option.py::test_team_section_command_entry_not_revealed`.
- **P3 (log redaction equality).** Most plausible wrong fix: display-only fix, no masker
  registration — `checks/sf-no-log-redaction.patch`; caught by
  `test_log_redaction.py::test_team_section_value_redacted` / `::test_team_env_var_value_redacted`.
  Opposite wrong fix (register every team value with the masker) — caught by
  `test_log_legit.py::test_team_non_sensitive_value_not_redacted`.

### Per trap (most plausible wrong fix → caught?)

- **T1** landing-only route strip (`sf-landing-only`) → yes, by listing/CLI/log tests; crash variant
  (`sf-crash-single-option`) → yes, `status < 500` asserts.
- **T2** fix route only, leave shared `as_dict` masking → yes, `test_api_listing.py` pair.
- **T3** file-section spelling only, env spelling untouched (`sf-no-env-spelling`) → yes,
  `test_api_listing.py::test_team_env_var_*`, `test_api_single_option.py::test_team_env_var_spelling_not_revealed`.
- **T4** hide in API but not in `config list` write path (`sf-no-cli-list`) → yes, `test_cli.py` pair.
- **T5** values only, not `_cmd`/`_secret` (`sf-no-cmd-secret`) → yes, the three cmd/secret tests.
- **T6** display fix only, no log registration (`sf-no-log-redaction`) → yes, `test_log_redaction.py`.
- **T7** keep `_PER_KEY_SENSITIVE_PREFIXES` keyed on literal section names (`sf-no-backend-kwargs`)
  → yes, `test_api_backend_kwargs.py`.
- **T8** keep the injected guard "skip only when the base value is already hidden"
  (`sf-no-team-view`) → yes, `test_api_team_view.py` pair.
- **T9** literal registry check in `get-value` (`sf-no-get-value`) → yes,
  `test_cli.py::test_get_value_hide_sensitive_hides_team_section_value`.
- **T10** split on first separator (`sf-first-separator`) → yes, the six separator tests (gate: 20/26).

### Wrong fixes no test catches (leniency gaps)

- **W1 — refusal instead of hiding on the single-option route.** Return 404 (or a 200 with the
  option omitted) for a team-scoped sensitive request. Every single-option security test asserts
  only `status < 500` + marker absence; no test pins 200 + `< hidden >` for a *team* sensitive
  option (`test_base_sensitive_option_still_hidden` pins only the base). `sf-crash-single-option`
  catches the 500 variant only. The "Hidden form" clause ("or it is left out") even tolerates this
  textually — see §5 C2.
- **W2 — team view masks registered options but not per-key synthetic ones.** `_apply_team_overrides`
  guarded by `is_sensitive_option` alone leaks `[team=secrets] backend_kwarg__…` /
  `[team=workers] secrets_backend_kwarg__…` overrides through `GET /config?team_name=`.
  `test_api_team_view.py` covers only registered options (`sentry_dsn`, `backend_kwargs`);
  the per-key tests hit the listing path only.
- **W3 — per-key masking covers only the `secrets`/`backend_kwarg__` half** of
  `_PER_KEY_SENSITIVE_PREFIXES`; the `workers`/`secrets_backend_kwarg__` half has no test, base or
  team. A fix hard-coding one prefix passes everything.
- **W4 — cmd/secret shapes hidden in the API but printed by the CLI.** All `_cmd`/`_secret` tests go
  through the API (listing + single option). `config list --hide-sensitive` and `get-value
  --hide-sensitive` with `sql_alchemy_conn_cmd` are untested.
- **W5 — get-value / section-filtered listing mishandle the env-var-reported spelling.**
  `get-value team_a _api_auth__jwt_secret --hide-sensitive` and `GET /config?section=team_a` for an
  env-supplied secret are untested (full listing and the odd spelling on the single-option route are
  tested).
- **W6 — log redaction covers resolved values but not team `_cmd`/`_secret` strings.** The redaction
  tests only pass resolved values through `redact()`; a masker registration that skips the reference
  strings violates P2 in the log channel untested.
- **W7 — text-format listing.** Only the single-option route is exercised with `text/plain`;
  text listings of team secrets ride on the JSON path in practice (low risk).
- **W8 — test-shaped name allow-lists.** Partially covered: a fix keyed to `team_a`/`data-eng`
  fails the separator tests, and one keyed to the tested names fails `ml_platform`/`a=b`; residual
  risk is a character-class allow-list that happens to cover exactly dash/underscore/`=` — the
  tests never use a name outside `[A-Za-z0-9_-=]` (e.g. spaces, dots, unicode).

## 3. Hints (instruction sentences that name/paraphrase machinery)

No file name, function name, identifier, trap name or flaw count appears in the instruction. The
closest paraphrases, with judgement:

1. *"Some options are registered as **sensitive**: database connection strings, the Fernet key, API
   and JWT secrets, secrets-backend parameters, and so on."* — paraphrases the sensitivity registry
   and the enumeration maps 1:1 onto the secret shapes used in tests (`sql_alchemy_conn`,
   `fernet_key`, `secret_key`, `jwt_secret`, `backend_kwargs`/`backend_kwarg__*`, `sentry_dsn` via
   "and so on"). Needed to define "sensitive"; "secrets-backend parameters" is the only textual
   cover for T7. Neutral alternative: *"Some options are registered as sensitive — credentials,
   keys, tokens and the parameters of the secrets backend."* Keep the secrets-backend phrase (T7
   fairness depends on it). **low risk — keep (optionally genericised).**
2. *"…it replaces each sensitive value with the text `< hidden >`, and it redacts those values from
   the logs it writes."* — system fact; required by the contracts and P3. **keep.**
3. *"…wherever Airflow shows configuration with sensitive values hidden — over the REST API, on the
   command line, in the configuration view for one team, or in the process logs — … for the whole
   configuration, one section, or one option."* — enumerates the four channels (including the
   injected team view, T8's surface) and the three API granularities. Scope, not fix approach; but
   it is a surface list pointing at the injected sibling. Neutral alternative that keeps fairness:
   *"…wherever Airflow shows configuration with sensitive values hidden, on every interface and at
   every granularity (the whole configuration, one section, or one option), in process output as
   well as on screen."* Removing "the configuration view for one team" entirely would leave T8 to
   the catch-all "wherever"; keeping a generic phrase preserves notice without naming the surface.
   **low risk — optional neutralisation; do not delete without replacing.**
4. *"This covers the command or secret reference a sensitive value is loaded from as well as the
   value itself."* — the only cover for T5's shape (base code deliberately shows `_cmd`/`_secret`
   entries, which would otherwise argue the opposite). Paraphrases the mechanism without naming
   `_cmd`/`_secret`. **keep — required for T5 fairness.**
5. *"This holds however the team-scoped value is supplied, and whatever the team is called."* —
   scope; sole cover for T3/T5 supply spellings and T10 names. As written "whatever the team is
   called" reads like rhetoric and is the only thing standing between a solver and six failing
   separator tests. Strengthen per §5 C1. **medium risk as-is.**
6. The `[team_a=database]` sample and the `GET /api/v2/config/section/…/option/…` request are the
   reported instance; *"The example above is one instance, not the full set of ways the properties
   can be violated"* plus *"do not special-case the example input"* de-special-case it. **keep.**
7. *"(such as the Kubernetes provider or `airflow config lint`)"* — names two real unmasked callers.
   Illustrative of the preservation clause; a solver could copy the phrasing but gains no fix
   location. Neutral alternative: *"(such as providers or CLI commands that consume configuration)"*.
   **low risk — optional.**

## 4. Realism of the trap code

- **T1–T5, T10** are the published upstream fix sites (per notes/manifest) in real code reachable
  from the real entry points (REST config API, `airflow config` CLI, import-time masking). Nothing
  planted. T3's reported spelling (`section=team_a`, `key=_api_auth__jwt_secret`) is odd but is the
  genuine behaviour of the shared loader's team env-var split.
- **T6** (core `mask_secrets` registers only base-section values) and **T7** (per-key kwarg masking
  keyed on literal `secrets`/`workers` section names) are real, unmodified upstream code with real
  entry points (process start-up; `GET /config`). Not planted. Note the "nothing leaks today"
  rationalisation exists only inside the `sf-published-only.patch` simulation, **not** in the tree
  the solver sees — good.
- **T8** (injected `GET /config?team_name=…`) is plausible maintainer code: the per-team view is a
  natural feature on top of `conf.getsection(team_name=)`, and the guard "skip when the base value
  is already hidden" is a natural but incomplete guard that looks complete because it defers to the
  already-masked listing. Reachable through the documented REST surface. Acceptable; it is an
  injected sibling, flagged for the record.
- **T9** (injected `get-value --hide-sensitive`) mirrors the existing `list --hide-sensitive` flag,
  including help text; natural and reachable via the CLI. Acceptable; flagged as injected.
- **T10** input shape (`[a=b=database]`, team name containing the separator) is the most exotic
  input in the task. It is justified only by "whatever the team is called" and by the fact the
  published fix resolves names right-to-left; a deployment whose team labels contain `=` is unusual
  but the feature treats names as opaque labels. Borderline-contrived, acceptable given the
  property wording — which is also why C1 below matters.

## 5. Verdict

**NOT READY.** No cheat-level hint was found (no file/function/identifier/flaw-count leak; the
example is properly de-special-cased), and the wrong-fix coverage is strong (15/15 declared patch
checks behave as catalogued). Two instruction/test mismatches must be closed before trial:

**C1 (required) — give real notice for arbitrary team names.** Six security tests
(`test_api_single_option.py::test_team_name_containing_separator_not_revealed`,
`test_api_listing.py::test_team_name_containing_separator_not_in_listing`,
`test_api_listing.py::test_team_name_containing_separator_section_filtered_not_in_listing`,
`test_api_backend_kwargs.py::test_team_name_containing_separator_backend_kwarg_not_in_listing`,
`test_cli.py::test_config_list_hides_team_name_containing_separator`,
`test_log_redaction.py::test_team_name_containing_separator_value_redacted`) plus one functional
test fail the natural implementation (split team/section on the first separator — exactly
`checks/sf-first-separator.patch`, gate 20/26). Their only cover is the phrase "whatever the team
is called". Replace that clause in property 1 with:

> "This holds however the team-scoped value is supplied, and whatever the team is called — team
> names are arbitrary labels, and nothing may be assumed about the characters a name contains."

(Neutral: does not name the separator or the split direction.)

**C2 (required) — reconcile "Hidden form" with the CLI section-header assertions.**
"A hidden value is reported the way it is today: the option stays listed and its value is
`< hidden >` (or it is left out)" sanctions leaving a hidden option out; the tests
`security/test_cli.py::test_config_list_hides_team_section_value` (line 17),
`::test_config_list_section_hides_team_section_value` (line 31),
`::test_config_list_hides_team_name_containing_separator` (line 45) additionally require the
section header to remain — a compliant "leave it out and don't print empty sections" fix fails them.
Pick one side; recommended: amend the contract (also closes wrong fix W1):

> "**Hidden form.** A hidden value is reported the way it is today: the option stays listed and its
> value is `< hidden >`, and the request still succeeds — hiding must not turn a request into an
> error or a not-found. A section stays listed even when every value in it is hidden."

(Alternative: delete the three header assertions, keeping only the marker-absence asserts. That
loosening would, however, admit section-dropping wrong fixes for all-sensitive sections.)

**Recommended test tightenings (leniency, non-blocking):** add (a) a team-view test for a per-key
`backend_kwarg__*` override (W2); (b) a `workers`/`secrets_backend_kwarg__` team case (W3);
(c) team `_cmd`/`_secret` entries through `config list --hide-sensitive` (W4); (d) a functional
assertion that a team-sensitive single-option request returns 200 with `< hidden >` (W1, pairs with
C2); (e) at least one team name with a character outside `[A-Za-z0-9_-=]` (W8).

With C1 and C2 applied and the gate re-run, the task is READY.
